```javascript
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const TemperatureConverter = () => {
  const [celsius, setCelsius] = useState(0);
  const [fahrenheit, setFahrenheit] = useState(32);

  const handleCelsiusChange = (e) => {
    setCelsius(parseFloat(e.target.value));
    setFahrenheit((celsius * 9 / 5) + 32);
  };

  const handleFahrenheitChange = (e) => {
    setFahrenheit(parseFloat(e.target.value));
    setCelsius((fahrenheit - 32) * 5 / 9);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      <label htmlFor="celsius">Celsius:</label>
      <input
        type="number"
        id="celsius"
        value={celsius}
        onChange={handleCelsiusChange}
      />
      <label htmlFor="fahrenheit">Fahrenheit:</label>
      <input
        type="number"
        id="fahrenheit"
        value={fahrenheit}
        onChange={handleFahrenheitChange}
      />
    </div>
  );
};

export default TemperatureConverter;
```

This `TemperatureConverter` component uses two state variables `celsius` and `fahrenheit`, along with their respective setters, to store the current temperature values in both units. The `handleCelsiusChange` and `handleFahrenheitChange` functions update the corresponding state variable when their input elements' values change. When one value is updated, the other value is calculated based on the conversion formula and updated accordingly.

